# ===== CONCEPTS =====
# R2_j regression = the X on the OTHER X's (Y is NOT in it). R2_j -> 1 = multicollinearity, SE blows up
# Unbiased = AVERAGE over many samples equals the true beta (not every sample)
# Omitted variable that affects Y AND is correlated with X -> BIASED (MLR4 fails). Same signs = up, opposite = down
# Random/ID/day-of-week variable omitted -> no bias. Y in $1000s, small n, high correlation -> NOT MLR4 problems
# Heteroskedasticity (MLR5) -> variance formula wrong, but STILL UNBIASED
# Perfect collinearity (MLR3) -> R shows NA (e.g. total = A + B). High correlation is OK (bigger SE)
# SLR3: X must vary (if everyone has educ = 12, slope can't be computed)
# Bigger SST (more spread in X, bigger n) or smaller sigma^2 -> smaller Var(b1). Intercept doesn't matter
# Residuals ALWAYS: average 0, uncorrelated with each X, line passes through the means. NOT uncorrelated with Y
# sigma-hat estimates sigma = sd of the ERROR u (not sd of Y, not R2). True sigma given -> "sd"; estimated -> "se"
# Level y x c -> every b x c.  Level x x c -> its b / c.  log(y) units change -> only the INTERCEPT changes
